// std.core.io
// the error every write can produce. it lives in core, not in
// std.text or std.env, because Display's signatures name it and
// core sits below both — an error type must be reachable from
// every layer that can fail

IoError* = Closed          // the sink is gone: a closed pipe, a dropped socket
          | Full           // no room: a fixed buffer, a full disk
          | Invalid        // the bytes are not what this sink accepts
          | Interrupted    // retryable; the write made no progress

// writing formatted text can fail two ways: the sink rejects it,
// or the buffer cannot grow. neither converts into the other —
// there is no From — so the honest type is the union, and .try()
// merges it into the caller's set for free
WriteError* = IoError | AllocError

// a sink is anything bytes can be written to. String is one, and
// so is Console — which is why `{}` formatting works into either
// without the format machinery knowing the difference.
//
// `write` speaks WriteError, the union, and that cost is accepted
// knowingly: a console can never produce AllocError and a growable
// String can never produce IoError, but there is no From, so one
// sink type cannot pretend the two are one error. `.try()` merges
// either into the caller's set for free, so the cost is paid only
// where someone actually matches on the error
Sink* = {
    write* = (self :: @Self, bytes: str) Res<(), WriteError>

    // one byte. The format writers build a number digit by digit, and a
    // digit has no `str` to point at: a str BORROWS bytes that already
    // exist somewhere, and the only way to make one is str_at, which needs
    // a Ptr, and the only source of a Ptr is an Alloc. A sink that could
    // take bytes but not a byte would therefore force the integer writers
    // to allocate — which is the exact cost this whole design exists to
    // avoid. So the floor is two calls wide, not one
    write_byte* = (self :: @Self, b: u8) Res<(), WriteError>
}
